The problem is about finding real numbers $a$ and $d$ for which the matrix
\begin{align*}
\begin{pmatrix} a & 3 \\ -8 & d \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
is its own inverse. This means that the matrix, when multiplied by itself, gives the identity matrix. Calculating the product of the matrix with itself, we get
\[\begin{pmatrix} a & 3 \\ -8 & d \end{pmatrix}^2 = \begin{pmatrix} a & 3 \\ -8 & d \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} a & 3 \\ -8 & d \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} a^2 - 24 & 3a + 3d \\ -8a - 8d & d^2 - 24 \end{pmatrix}\]
Setting this equal to the identity matrix $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$, we have the system of equations $a^2 - 24 = 1$ and $3a + 3d = 0$, as well as $-8a - 8d = 0$ and $d^2 - 24 = 1$. Solving the first equation $a^2 - 24 = 1$ for $a$, we get $a = \pm5$. Solving the second equation $3a + 3d = 0$ for $d$, we get $d = -a$. Therefore, the possible pairs $(a, d)$ are $(5, -5)$ and $(-5, 5)$. This means there are 2 possible pairs of $(a, d)$. </think> <answer>$\boxed{2}$ </answer>
